  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nA very nice day is underway this afternoon as a much cooler and \ndrier airmass has settled in across eastern Kansas. Currently, the \nupper level ridge has pushed south across the southern Plains with \nnorthwesterly flow over the central and northern Plains and \nmonsoonal moisture dominating much of the mountain-west. For the \nremainder of the day today, expect temperatures to warm a few \ndegrees further before topping out in the low to mid 80s. With \ndewpoints mixing out into the upper 50s, this will make for very \nnice weather for any outdoor activities. \n\nA more active pattern for rain and storms will move in overnight \ntonight as vorticity rounding the mid-level ridge axis moves into \ncentral Kansas. The increase in mid-level lift and moisture should \ngive way to scattered to widespread showers and a few thunderstorms \nfrom early to late Thursday morning. Model guidance is showing PWATs \nin this timeframe to be around 2\" so some heavy rainfall will be \npossible within the strongest showers/storms. Most areas across far \neastern KS will likely see less than an inch of rainfall through \nThursday morning, but areas across north-central and central KS \ncould see totals of 1-2 inches if storms train over areas for long \nenough. Mid-level lift begins to shift into Missouri by the \nafternoon Thursday and help provide a period of drier weather for \nmuch of the afternoon. Given the amount of cloud cover and rain \nchances through the morning, high temperatures Thursday will likely \nstruggle to reach above the mid 80s.\n\nThe next round of rain/storms moves in later Thursday night with a \nmore robust shortwave trough rounding the upper ridge. Widespread \nlow and mid-level lift increases across north-central and \nnortheastern Kansas into early Friday morning. Given the very moist \nairmass characterized by 2-2.25\" PWATs, showers and storms will be \ncapable of very efficient rainfall rates Friday morning. Recent \nguidance depicts the nose of the nocturnal LLJ to position itself \nacross far north-central KS around midnight Friday morning before \nslowly turning and moving east towards northeastern Kansas through \nthe morning hours. This seems to be the area best positioned for \ncontinued development of showers/storms as the mid-level wave passes \noverhead and thus, the area of heaviest rainfall. NBM probabilities \ngive areas north of I-70 widespread >50% chances for over 1 inch of \nrainfall Friday morning. This continues further as areas closer to \nHwy 36 are highlighted by a 40-60% chance for rainfall totals \ngreater than 2 inches. The excessive rainfall could lead to \nlocalized flooding Friday morning. Precipitation chances come to an \nend by Friday afternoon as subsidence on the backside of the \nshortwave builds in from the northwest. Again, cloud cover sticking \naround for much of the daytime hours Friday will help to limit \ntemperatures by the afternoon - likely only reaching the mid 80s.\n\nHot and mostly dry conditions are looking more likely Saturday \nthrough early next week as the upper level ridge builds north across \nthe central Plains. This will keep most of the precipitation chances \nnorth of the area as afternoon high temperatures return to the mid \nto upper 90s - possibly reaching triple digits Sunday and Monday. \nSome low-end precipitation chances may come by early to mid next \nweek, but confidence is low as energy rounding the ridge will likely \nstay across Nebraska.",
